1.93 - ReWrote all ANSI files as they were riddled with mistakes like
       literally dozens of "{esc}]s^M{esc}]u" which means "save this location's
       address, issue a [RETURN], restore old location".  As you can see 
       at best this is just a loop and at worst (for BBSs that interpret
       all ANSI charactors correctly) it screws up the local and maybe remote
       terminals rendering the game unplayable.  Not sure how this made it
       thru beta testing.

     - Rewrote several of the loops in the main AREXX program to be faster.
       Several double loops were elimated.

     - ReWrote the main AREXX program to use IPL commands.  What the heck
       Excelsior! is gonna look for 'em anyway.  Also make the code easier
       for me to read.

     - Took a few of the large text blocks out of the main AREXX program and
       made them SENDFILEs to reduce the size of the main AREXX program.

     - Reduced it to an 8 color game as many Amigans with 7MHz machines
       can't run a 16 color screen that is fast enough to be pretty. 
       I don't think this takes much from the game at all.
  
     - Introduced 1/50 second pauses on some of the animations to make them
       seeable on a 14400 modem.  If you find them offensive just eliminate
       the {slash}s1 commands in the ANSI/#? files

     - Eliminated the need to create a GAME: assignment.  I wish programmers
       would stop relying on assignments as I already have 10,000 of them. 
       When did path names become so TABOO?  To enter your path name (or 
       assignment if you must) look in the main AREXX program for the line:
    
             PATH='DH2:BBS/DOORS/CYBER/'

       and change it to whatever you wish your path to be.

     - Elimated about 32K from the size of the AREXX program.  It still
       is a pretty fat little program but I think I'll only get about
       20K more out of it.  Still that's about 83K down from 130+K.

     - The MAINT AREXX program was worked on a little but it still won't 
       restart the game properly.  If someone can see the error please
       leave me a note or give me a call.

     - Took the SIGNS out of the code until I figger em out.  It was
       telling me that there was a sign every time I moved but there
       was never anything written on it.  A bug in the MAINT program
       when it creates the MAP file is the problem I think.

                        - - C Y B E R M E C H  7.0 0  D O C S - -

This is the latest updated version of CYBERMECH, an on-line game playable thru
TRANSAMIGA BBS SYSTEMS.  CYBERMECH is a computerized board game played mostly 
with intellect and imagination.  For those already familiar with CYBERMECH, we
have added enhanced ANSI graphics to our screens to add a more
realistic experience.  

This game is for TRANSAMIGA BBS users only, and allows players to simulate an
operation of a Mech similar to Robotech and Mech Warrior role-playing
games.  With over three years of development, CYBERMECH is one of the
most popular TA games ever released.  The following is a set up guide for
how to install, maintain, and operate CYBERMECH gaming system on your 
system.

I       What to use the run the game:  

        1.  One meg of ram (at the very least.)
        2.  Hard disk.
        3.  Cron-utility program (TPT Cron, etc.)
        4.  AREXX version 1.15 or better.
        
II.     How to use this Archive File to install CYBERMECH:

        1.  Assign a directory called "Game:" - put this anywhere on your
            hard drive, e.g.:  "assign game: dh0:game" 

        2.  Make a directory called "ANSI" in your "game" directory.

        3.  Place all of the files in this achive in the "game" directory 
            and all of the files in the "ANSI" archive in the "game:ansi"
            directory.

        4.  Take CYBERMECH.TRANS, put it in your "REXX" directory & create
            an entry for it in your "EXTRAS.RX" file per standard 
            TRANSAMIGA external command launching.  (See TRANSAMIGA
            manual.)

III.    Setting up CYBERMECH for role-playing:

        **NOTE: CYBERMECH will automatically create game files if they do
          not currently exist. CYBERMECH fully maintains these files and
          requires little or no intervention on the part of the SYSOP.

        1.  From your GAME directory  run the program MAINT2.REXX.
            a.  Select option regenerate map.
            b.  This will create a new map file for your game.  When it asks 
                do you want to create a new map, answer "yes."
            c.  After map generation is done, (which will take up to five 
                minutes), select option "To place pirates on map."  Enter
                the number of priates to put in the map, 50 is the median
                generally used.  After selecting the number, the pirates
                will be created onto the map file. 
             d. Select option to create the city mainframe.  This will prompt
                you for several different passwords that the players will 
                discover later in the game.  Don't make these passwords too
                complex, the users like to be able to "discover" these
                passwords on their own.  (This, of course, is up to the
                discretion of the SYSOP, but we have found it is easier
                to make the "hackers" happy by "allowing" them to guess on 
                their own.)  
             e. Select option to place Cryon parts. These are the 4 pieces
                necessary to win the game.  Enter a number between 1 and
                625.  Note: place these fairly spaced apart, rather than
                grouped together.  This will make them harder to find.

IV.             The MAINT2 doc program:
                
                CYBERMECH's latest addition is the MAINT2 program, which is
                an automagick maintenance program for CYBERMECH, which will do
                the following for you:

                ***Check the map for the number of plays & regenerate the map
                   randomly placing ore, terrain or such on the map, but will
                   not move players or their possessions. 

                ***Parse the map for non-existent, dead or incorrect player 
                   info & correct it accordingly.

                ***Parse all player files and maintain the player variables.

                ***Flag week-old players as inactive & delete 15-day old
                   players.  Thereby, only the serious CYBERMECH players
                   will be kept on the board.
                                
                To activate MAINT2 in automatic mode, the parameter is:
                AUTO, e.g.: "RX MAINT2.REXX auto".      

                When MAINT is run in automatic mode, nothing is passed
                to the screen, neither does it require any input from you.
                This was designed to be run in a Cron once nightly &
                will keep your CYBERMECH files from becoming too large or
                corrupt.  Of course, if you want your system to be corrupted,
                by all means do not use automatic mode.     

       W A R N I N G ! ! ! ! 

The following files are necessary for operation of the game and should not be
modified or deleted:

**Mech1
**999
**1000
**Sooth and Sooth2
**Instruct.ANSI and Instruct

Tips:

10 Mech files are included with this Archives.  You have to use Mech1,
but you don't have to use Mech2 thru Mech10, you can create them
thru the Maint2 program if you want to.

We hope that those of you who have previously run CYBERMECH find
the new ANSI graphics a bonus by attracting more users & by 
making the game more interesting and imaginative.   If you have not yet run
CYBERMECH on your TRANSAMIGA board try it for a month and see what
your response is.  If you like what you see, we would appreciate any type
of positive feed back through the board, by voice or by mail.  We are 
updating Cybermech on a constant basis to please our users and yours.
The great and noble cause of a running a pirate-free bulletin board must be 
continued through shareware!!!  If you would like to help us enhance
our graphics, storyline, maps and mechs, please send a contribution
of at least $7.50 to:

